window.EXAM={"id": "exam-2", "name": "Practice Exam 2", "stimuli": {"T1": {"html": "<p>A student in the Ortiz household is attending a live video class on a laptop, and the video keeps freezing. The household has a 100 Mbps internet plan. The student records the following.</p>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Device</th><th>Current activity</th><th>Bandwidth in use</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Student laptop</td><td>Live video class</td><td>8 Mbps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Desktop computer</td><td>Downloading a 60 GB game update</td><td>70 Mbps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smart TV</td><td>Streaming a movie in 4K</td><td>18 Mbps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tablet</td><td>Idle</td><td>0 Mbps</td></tr></table>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Student laptop</th><th>Reading</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Wi-Fi signal</td><td>4 of 4 bars</td></tr><tr><td>CPU</td><td>35%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Memory (RAM)</td><td>41% of 8 GB in use</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>38% free</td></tr></table>"}, "T2": {"html": "<p>The owner of Sunrise Bakery asked a technician to document the bakery's network. The technician recorded the following. All wired devices connect to ports on the wireless gateway's built-in switch.</p>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Device</th><th>Connection</th><th>MAC address</th><th>IP address</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Wireless gateway</td><td>Coaxial cable to ISP</td><td>00:1A:2B:3C:4D:01</td><td>LAN 192.168.0.1 / WAN 198.51.100.73</td></tr>\n<tr><td>POS terminal</td><td>Wired, port 1</td><td>B4:7C:9C:10:22:31</td><td>192.168.0.20 (static)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Office PC</td><td>Wired, port 2</td><td>6C:4B:90:5E:01:AF</td><td>192.168.0.35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Receipt printer</td><td>Wired, port 3</td><td>00:80:77:4A:12:0C</td><td>192.168.0.21 (static)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Owner's tablet</td><td>Wireless</td><td>3A:F1:22:8C:07:5D</td><td>192.168.0.112</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smart oven</td><td>Wireless</td><td>D8:28:C9:61:4E:B2</td><td>192.168.0.140</td></tr></table>"}, "T3": {"html": "<p>Oak Lane Tutoring uses one wireless router for its office computer, which stores student records, and for the personal devices that parents and students bring to sessions. A technician views the router's administration page.</p>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Current value</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Administrator login</td><td>admin / factory-default password</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Firmware</td><td>v2.0.4 (update v2.3.1 available: fixes a known security vulnerability)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wireless security mode</td><td>WEP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wi-Fi Protected Setup (WPS)</td><td>Enabled</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Guest network</td><td>Disabled</td></tr></table>"}, "T4": {"html": "<p>A team is planning the network for the Harbor City Science Fair, held in two exhibit halls separated by a concrete wall. The team assumes that each wireless access point (WAP) supports 40 mobile devices.</p>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>User group</th><th>Number</th><th>Tasks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Judges</td><td>12</td><td>Walk among exhibits and enter scores in a web form</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Livestream crew</td><td>3</td><td>Stream the awards ceremony to the internet with no interruptions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Event staff</td><td>4</td><td>Manage the event from a management workstation; host files on a local server</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Visitors</td><td>200 smartphones</td><td>Basic internet access only: 130 in Hall A and 70 in Hall B</td></tr></table>"}, "T5": {"html": "<p>Pinecrest Middle School has a teacher segment (VLAN 10, 192.168.10.0/24, gateway 192.168.10.1) and a student segment (VLAN 20, 192.168.20.0/24, gateway 192.168.20.1). A teacher reports that laptop T-114, which was configured manually last week, can print to the teacher printer at 192.168.10.30 but cannot reach the internet or the student segment's file server. Another teacher's laptop, T-115, works normally.</p>\n<pre>T-114&gt; ipconfig\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.10.57\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.1.1\n\nT-115&gt; ipconfig\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.10.84\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.10.1</pre>"}, "T6": {"html": "<p>A technician at a grocery chain needs the point-of-sale (POS) logs from a log server and enters the following commands.</p>\n<pre>mreyes@tech-laptop ~ $ pwd\n/home/mreyes\nmreyes@tech-laptop ~ $ cd reports\nmreyes@tech-laptop ~/reports $ sftp logs@10.12.0.40\nlogs@10.12.0.40's password:\nConnected to 10.12.0.40.\nsftp&gt; pwd\nRemote working directory: /var/log/pos\nsftp&gt; ls\npos-0612.log   pos-0613.log   pos-0614.log\nsftp&gt;</pre>"}, "T7": {"html": "<p>The following is part of the routing table on router R1 in a company's network.</p>\n<pre>Destination      Next hop        Interface  Metric\n10.40.1.0/24     directly conn.  Gi0/0      0\n10.40.2.0/24     10.40.255.2     Gi0/1      2\n10.40.2.0/24     10.40.254.2     Gi0/2      5\n0.0.0.0/0        203.0.113.1     Gi0/3      1</pre>"}}, "questions": [{"id": 1, "lo": "1.2.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "Based on the information, which of the following is the most likely cause of the frozen video?", "opts": ["The household's available bandwidth is nearly used up.", "The laptop's processor is overloaded.", "The laptop's wireless signal is too weak.", "The laptop does not have enough free storage."], "ans": "A", "expl": "The devices are using 96 of 100 Mbps (8 + 70 + 18), so little bandwidth is left for the live class. The laptop's CPU, RAM, storage, and signal readings are all normal.", "set": "T1"}, {"id": 2, "lo": "1.1.A", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "An AI assistant tells the student to buy a new laptop with more RAM. Which of the following best evaluates this suggestion?", "opts": ["It should be followed, because AI tools analyze diagnostic data more accurately than people.", "It is not supported by the data, and simpler, low-cost solutions should be tried first.", "It should be followed, because more RAM always improves video quality.", "It is supported by the data, because the laptop is using 41% of its RAM."], "ans": "B", "expl": "AI suggestions must be checked against the diagnostic data and human judgment. RAM use is well below the 80–90% warning level, and simple, nondisruptive fixes such as pausing the large download should be tried before costly hardware changes.", "set": "T1"}, {"id": 3, "lo": "1.1.B", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "A tablet displays the message \"Storage almost full: 2.1 GB of 64 GB available,\" and apps are slow to open saved files. Which of the following is the most likely root cause?", "opts": ["Sustained high CPU usage", "Insufficient available storage", "A weak wireless signal", "Overheating of the battery"], "ans": "B", "expl": "2.1 ÷ 64 ≈ 3.3% free, below the 10% level at which a device may not have enough space to perform reliably, which slows file access.", "set": null}, {"id": 4, "lo": "1.1.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "A student's laptop shuts down repeatedly while the student works in a car parked in direct sunlight, and the laptop displayed a temperature warning before each shutdown. Which of the following is the most appropriate solution?", "opts": ["Moving the laptop to a cooler, shaded area with good airflow", "Installing additional RAM so the laptop runs more efficiently", "Moving the laptop closer to a wireless access point", "Deleting temporary files to free space on the laptop"], "ans": "A", "expl": "The symptoms point to overheating. Solutions include checking ventilation, ending high-usage tasks, and moving the device to a cooler area out of direct sunlight.", "set": null}, {"id": 5, "lo": "1.2.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A user connects a laptop to a protected wireless network. Which of the following best verifies that the connection was successful?", "opts": ["Confirming that the network's SSID appears in the list of available networks", "Confirming that the wireless adapter is turned on in the laptop's network settings", "Checking that the password was accepted without displaying an error message", "Checking the network icon for errors and confirming that websites and resources load"], "ans": "D", "expl": "After connecting, users should verify the connection by checking the network icon for errors and confirming access to the internet and network resources. Seeing the SSID, having the adapter on, or an accepted password happen before or during the connection, not after.", "set": null}, {"id": 6, "lo": "1.3.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "Credentials from a student's compromised online shopping account are sold to another person. Which of the following is the most likely impact on the student?", "opts": ["Fraudulent purchases or loans that damage the student's credit", "A slower internet connection on the student's home network for weeks", "Loss of the student's home wireless network password to the attacker", "A permanent change to the MAC address of the student's laptop"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Information from compromised accounts can be used to commit fraud, make purchases, or apply for loans, leading to credit score damage and long-term identity misuse.", "set": null}, {"id": 7, "lo": "1.3.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "A student logs in to a bank account on a library computer placed next to the library's busy entrance. Which of the following security controls best addresses the risk of shoulder surfing?", "opts": ["Installing antivirus software and scanning the computer for malware", "Enabling email filtering on the student's bank and school accounts", "Installing a privacy screen and turning the monitor away from the entrance", "Keeping the computer's operating system and browser fully updated"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Shoulder surfing is someone viewing a screen without permission. Privacy screens limit viewing angles, and screens should be positioned away from high-traffic areas. The other controls address malware and phishing.", "set": null}, {"id": 8, "lo": "1.4.A", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A school's IT department is revising its password policy. Which of the following changes would most increase the time that an automated tool needs to find a user's password?", "opts": ["Requiring users to include their birth year somewhere in each password", "Raising the minimum length from 8 to 14 characters and requiring all four character sets", "Allowing passwords made only of lowercase dictionary words that are easy to remember", "Requiring users to reuse the same password on every school system to avoid lockouts"], "ans": "B", "expl": "The longer and more complex a password is, the longer an automated tool takes to find it. Personal information, dictionary words, and reuse all make passwords easier to guess or more damaging if exposed.", "set": null}, {"id": 9, "lo": "1.4.B", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A student receives a text message that appears to be from the school district. It says the student's account will be closed unless the student taps a link and signs in within one hour. Which of the following is the most appropriate response?", "opts": ["Tapping the link right away so that the account is not closed by the district", "Replying to the message with the account password to confirm the student's identity", "Contacting the district through its official website or phone number", "Forwarding the message to classmates so that their accounts are not closed either"], "ans": "C", "expl": "To prevent phishing-based compromise, users should verify message sources, inspect links before clicking, and contact organizations directly through official websites or phone numbers. Urgency is a common phishing tactic.", "set": null}, {"id": 10, "lo": "1.4.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "An adversary in a parking lot captures wireless traffic from a shop's network. Which of the following router settings would make the captured data most difficult for the adversary to read?", "opts": ["Hiding the network's SSID", "Lowering the router's transmit power", "Enabling WPA3 encryption", "Changing the router's administrator username"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Encrypting data before transmission makes captured data hard to read, and WPA3 is the recommended level. Hiding the SSID, lowering power, and changing the admin username do not encrypt the traffic.", "set": null}, {"id": 11, "lo": "2.1.A", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "A technician checks the network configuration of a laptop that cannot reach any network resources. The only IPv4 address assigned is 127.0.0.1. Which of the following is the most likely explanation?", "opts": ["The DHCP server assigned the laptop an APIPA address.", "The laptop has a public address assigned by the internet service provider.", "The laptop is not connected to any network, possibly because its adapter is disabled.", "The laptop's default gateway is set to the wrong address."], "ans": "C", "expl": "A loopback address of 127.0.0.1 means the device can only send data to itself and may not be connected to any network. The solutions are making sure Wi-Fi and the network adapter are enabled and restarting the device. APIPA addresses begin with 169.254.", "set": null}, {"id": 12, "lo": "2.1.B", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "A student's computer is connected wirelessly. Running ping 8.8.8.8 shows an average response time of 245 ms and 3% packet loss while family members are streaming and downloading. Which of the following is the most appropriate solution?", "opts": ["Releasing and renewing the computer's IP address", "Enabling the computer's network adapter and restarting the computer", "Replacing the router's power cable", "Changing the computer's wireless connection to a wired connection"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Response times over 100 ms or more than 1–2% packet loss indicate congestion or poor performance. Solutions include switching from wireless to wired, pausing large downloads, and moving closer to the access point. Release/renew is for APIPA, and enabling the adapter is for loopback-only results.", "set": null}, {"id": 13, "lo": "2.2.D", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "The technician will draw a network diagram from the table. Which of the following correctly describes how the connections should be drawn?", "opts": ["Dashed lines for the POS terminal, office PC, and receipt printer; solid lines for the tablet and smart oven", "Solid lines for all devices, because every device connects to the same gateway", "Solid lines for the POS terminal, office PC, and receipt printer; dashed lines for the tablet and smart oven", "Dashed lines for the devices with static addresses; solid lines for all other devices"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Network diagrams use solid lines for wired connections and dashed lines for wireless connections, and they include MAC and IP addresses to help troubleshooting and expansion.", "set": "T2"}, {"id": 14, "lo": "2.2.C", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "Which of the following addresses in the table is a public IP address?", "opts": ["192.168.0.1", "192.168.0.20", "192.168.0.140", "198.51.100.73"], "ans": "D", "expl": "The gateway's WAN address, 198.51.100.73, is outside the private ranges (10.x, 172.16–31.x, 192.168.x) and is assigned by the ISP to communicate on the internet. Every 192.168.0.x address is private and used only inside the LAN.", "set": "T2"}, {"id": 15, "lo": "2.2.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "The POS terminal sends data to the receipt printer. Which of the following best describes how the gateway's built-in switch delivers the data?", "opts": ["It uses its CAM table to forward the data only to port 3.", "It broadcasts the data to every port so that the printer can find it.", "It sends the data to the ISP, which returns it to the printer.", "It uses the printer's public IP address to find the correct port."], "ans": "A", "expl": "A switch stores the MAC addresses of connected devices in its content addressable memory (CAM) table and forwards data only to the port of the destination MAC address (00:80:77:4A:12:0C on port 3) instead of broadcasting to all ports.", "set": "T2"}, {"id": 16, "lo": "2.2.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "Which of the following is the dotted decimal form of the binary IPv4 address 11000000.10101000.00000001.00001010?", "opts": ["192.168.1.5", "192.168.1.10", "192.168.10.1", "193.168.1.10"], "ans": "B", "expl": "11000000 = 128 + 64 = 192; 10101000 = 128 + 32 + 8 = 168; 00000001 = 1; 00001010 = 8 + 2 = 10. The address is 192.168.1.10.", "set": null}, {"id": 17, "lo": "2.5.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "Parents' and students' personal devices join the same network as the office computer. Which of the following is the most likely impact of this arrangement?", "opts": ["The router's firmware would update itself more often because of the extra devices.", "The office computer would lose its MAC address when guests connect.", "An unknown device with weak security could be used to move laterally and reach the student records.", "The guests' devices would be unable to receive IP addresses from the router."], "ans": "C", "expl": "Lateral movement often happens through insecure IoT devices or unknown guest devices with weak credentials or unmonitored access. With no guest network, visitors share the segment that holds the student records.", "set": "T3"}, {"id": 18, "lo": "2.5.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "Which of the following sets of changes best mitigates the vulnerabilities shown?", "opts": ["Keep WEP, hide the SSID, and leave WPS enabled so that guests can connect quickly without a password", "Install firmware v2.3.1 and keep every other setting, because the update fixes all of the vulnerabilities", "Rename the SSID and keep the default admin password, because attackers cannot guess a new SSID name", "Change the default admin password, use WPA2 or WPA3, disable WPS, update the firmware, and enable a guest network"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Controls for SOHO vulnerabilities are changing default credentials, enabling WPA2 or WPA3, disabling WPS, installing firmware updates, and separating unknown devices on a guest network. Each other choice leaves at least one vulnerability in place.", "set": "T3"}, {"id": 19, "lo": "2.3.A", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A food truck owner needs a device that processes card payments and keeps track of inventory. Which of the following endpoint devices is most appropriate?", "opts": ["A network-attached storage device", "A point-of-sale (POS) terminal", "A smart speaker", "A voice over IP (VoIP) phone"], "ans": "B", "expl": "POS terminals are specialized endpoints for transaction processing and inventory management. VoIP phones handle voice calls, NAS devices store files, and smart speakers are IoT devices.", "set": null}, {"id": 20, "lo": "2.3.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A small business must connect its main office to a warehouse 600 meters away. The link must carry very high bandwidth, and it runs past heavy machinery that produces strong electromagnetic interference. Which of the following transmission media is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Coaxial cable", "Fiber optic cable", "Shielded twisted pair (STP)", "Wireless (Wi-Fi)"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Fiber optic cable supports 1–100 Gbps over distances up to 100 km and is immune to EMI because the signal is carried by light. Twisted pair is limited to 100 meters, coaxial loses significant signal over 450 meters, and wireless is more susceptible to interference.", "set": null}, {"id": 21, "lo": "2.3.C", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A dental office has no dedicated IT staff. Its 25 employees connect laptops that come and go during the day. Which of the following best explains why dynamic addressing is appropriate for the laptops?", "opts": ["DHCP assigns addresses automatically to connected devices, which is easy to manage and efficient.", "Dynamic addresses never change, so each laptop can always be found at the same address on the network.", "Dynamic addressing requires each address to be tracked by hand to prevent duplicate address conflicts.", "DHCP gives each laptop a public address that customers can reach directly from the internet."], "ans": "A", "expl": "Dynamic addressing suits mobile or temporary devices and networks with limited administrative support; DHCP assigns addresses only to devices currently connected. Static addressing is the one that stays constant and requires manual tracking.", "set": null}, {"id": 22, "lo": "2.4.A", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A family wants an AI tool to suggest an upgrade for its home network. Which of the following prompts is most likely to produce a practical recommendation?", "opts": ["\"Design a home network for 4 users with 3 laptops, 2 game consoles, and a smart TV, with evening streaming, a 2,000-square-foot house, and a $250 budget.\"", "\"Design the fastest and most secure home network possible, using the newest equipment available.\"", "\"What router do most families buy? List the top models and their prices from this year.\"", "\"List every network device a family could buy, including switches, routers, servers, and firewalls.\""], "ans": "A", "expl": "Effective prompts include details such as the number of users, types of devices, internet usage patterns, and budget. Vague prompts may lead to impractical or overly complex recommendations.", "set": null}, {"id": 23, "lo": "2.4.B", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "A student configures a gaming PC as a media server for the household. Which of the following best verifies that the media server is configured correctly?", "opts": ["Confirming that the media server software is installed and running on the gaming PC", "Checking that the gaming PC has a wired Ethernet connection to the home router", "Running ipconfig on the gaming PC to confirm that its IP address has not changed", "Connecting from another device and confirming that it can open the shared files and media"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Verification of a media server means connecting to it from another device and confirming access to shared files, media, or games. Installing software and using a wired connection are configuration steps, not verification.", "set": null}, {"id": 24, "lo": "2.6.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "Which of the following best describes a benefit of dividing a small office network into segments?", "opts": ["It removes the need for passwords on the devices that are inside each segment.", "It gives every device in the network its own public IP address from the ISP.", "It allows all devices to communicate with each other without any restrictions.", "It isolates high-risk devices, reduces unnecessary traffic, and creates security zones."], "ans": "D", "expl": "Network segmentation divides a network into smaller segments that isolate sensitive or high-risk devices and data, reduce unnecessary traffic, and create distinct security zones.", "set": null}, {"id": 25, "lo": "2.6.B", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "Which of the following device groupings is based on the type of user?", "opts": ["Office computers, IoT devices, and guest devices", "Teachers, students, and technicians", "Gaming consoles, media servers, and video-editing computers", "Wired devices and wireless devices"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Grouping by user type, such as office departments or teachers, students, and technicians, reduces unnecessary traffic and simplifies access control. Office, IoT, and guest groups are based on risk level, and gaming, media, and editing groups are based on performance needs.", "set": null}, {"id": 26, "lo": "3.1.A", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "Which of the following devices is most appropriate for the judges?", "opts": ["Gaming desktops", "Desktop workstations with wired connections", "Video-editing laptops", "Tablets"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Tablets and smartphones are suitable for mobile access to forms or dashboards to view and update data quickly, which fits judges walking among exhibits.", "set": "T4"}, {"id": 27, "lo": "3.1.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "Which of the following groups should use wired connections?", "opts": ["Judges and visitors", "Visitors and livestream crew", "Judges and event staff", "Livestream crew and event staff"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Wired connections are for devices that need high reliability, low latency, or large data transfers, including management workstations, servers, and livestreaming equipment. Judges and visitors are mobile and need wireless access.", "set": "T4"}, {"id": 28, "lo": "3.1.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "What is the minimum number of WAPs needed to serve the visitors' smartphones?", "opts": ["4", "5", "6", "8"], "ans": "C", "expl": "The concrete wall means each hall needs its own WAPs. Hall A: 130 ÷ 40 = 3.25 → 4. Hall B: 70 ÷ 40 = 1.75 → 2. Total 6. Dividing 200 by 40 gives 5, which ignores the wall.", "set": "T4"}, {"id": 29, "lo": "3.6.B", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "Which of the following is the most likely root cause of T-114's problem?", "opts": ["The DHCP pool for VLAN 10 is exhausted.", "The default gateway on T-114 is incorrect.", "The subnet mask on T-114 is incorrect.", "A firewall rule is blocking traffic to the printer."], "ans": "B", "expl": "T-114's gateway, 192.168.1.1, is not on its 192.168.10.0/24 network, so it cannot send traffic off its segment. Local printing still works because the printer is on the same network. The mask matches T-115's, and the laptop has a valid address, so DHCP is not the issue.", "set": "T5"}, {"id": 30, "lo": "3.6.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "Which of the following is the most appropriate solution?", "opts": ["Change T-114's default gateway to 192.168.10.1.", "Change T-114's IP address to 192.168.1.57.", "Move T-114 to VLAN 20.", "Restart the teacher printer."], "ans": "A", "expl": "The solution for a missing or incorrect gateway is assigning the correct default gateway, here the router's address on VLAN 10. Changing the IP address to 192.168.1.57 would break local printing, and moving VLANs places a teacher device on the student segment.", "set": "T5"}, {"id": 31, "lo": "3.3.A", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "After the fix, which of the following best verifies that T-114 is configured correctly?", "opts": ["Printing a test page on the teacher printer to confirm that the laptop is connected", "Checking that T-114's MAC address appears in the output of the ipconfig /all command", "Restarting T-114 and confirming that it powers on and reaches the sign-in screen", "Running ipconfig to confirm the new gateway, then pinging internal and external addresses"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Verification means checking that the IP configuration matches the intended settings and then confirming that the device reaches both internal and external resources. Printing worked even before the fix, so it proves nothing.", "set": "T5"}, {"id": 32, "lo": "3.2.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A router receives a packet whose destination is a device on a different segment. Which of the following best describes what the router does?", "opts": ["It sends the packet back to the source, because routers connect a network only to the internet.", "It broadcasts the packet out of every interface to every segment until a device replies.", "It compares the destination IP address with its routing table and forwards the packet to the correct segment.", "It replaces the destination MAC address with the router's public IP address and forwards it."], "ans": "C", "expl": "A router's routing table shows possible routes. The router compares the destination IP address with the entries, determines the correct segment, and forwards the packet to a switch on that segment for delivery.", "set": null}, {"id": 33, "lo": "3.2.B", "skill": "1.B", "stem": "A café wants staff and customers to use the same wireless access point, but staff traffic must be kept separate from customer traffic. Which of the following segmentation methods is most appropriate?", "opts": ["Creating multiple SSIDs that are assigned to separate VLANs", "Building a separate physical network with its own router for staff", "Giving staff devices static IP addresses on the same segment as customers", "Connecting every staff device to a mesh topology"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Wireless segmentation using multiple SSIDs fits when different user groups need separate wireless access; the SSIDs can be assigned to separate VLANs or subnets for isolation and access control. Physical separation is more costly than needed.", "set": null}, {"id": 34, "lo": "3.2.C", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A small school with limited IT resources chooses a star topology for its classroom network. Which of the following is a disadvantage of this choice?", "opts": ["The topology requires a direct cable from every node to every other node.", "The topology is difficult to scale and troubleshoot.", "The central switch is a single point of failure.", "The topology is the most expensive to install."], "ans": "C", "expl": "A star is cost-effective, easy to install, scale, and troubleshoot, which suits limited resources, but all traffic passes through the central switch, making it a single point of failure. A cable from every node to every other node, and the highest cost, describe mesh.", "set": null}, {"id": 35, "lo": "3.3.B", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "Which of the following pairs of devices are on the same network?", "opts": ["10.1.1.70/26 and 10.1.1.130/26", "172.16.3.9/24 and 172.16.4.9/24", "192.168.5.10/25 and 192.168.5.130/25", "192.168.12.40/27 and 192.168.12.60/27"], "ans": "D", "expl": "A /27 block is 32 addresses, so .40 and .60 are both in 192.168.12.32–63 with matching network bits. For the other pairs: /26 splits .70 (block .64–127) from .130 (block .128–191); under /24 the third octets 3 and 4 differ; and /25 splits .10 (block .0–127) from .130 (block .128–255).", "set": null}, {"id": 36, "lo": "3.3.C", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "A technician unboxes a new managed switch and needs to set its password, IP address, and subnet mask before connecting it to the LAN. Which of the following is the most appropriate way to begin?", "opts": ["Plug the switch into the internet and wait for the ISP to configure it.", "Connect to the switch's management console with a console cable and use its CLI or GUI.", "Use ping to send the settings to the switch.", "Run ipconfig /renew on a laptop connected to the switch."], "ans": "B", "expl": "Switch settings such as the password, IP address, and subnet mask can be set manually through the switch's CLI or GUI management console, which is reached by connecting a console cable.", "set": null}, {"id": 37, "lo": "3.4.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "Students at a school must print to printers on the library segment, but they must not reach any other library systems. Which of the following controls best meets this requirement?", "opts": ["A single subnet for the student and library devices", "A guest SSID with no password for the student devices", "A deny-by-default policy between the subnets that allows only printing traffic", "A rule that allows all traffic from students during school hours"], "ans": "C", "expl": "To limit lateral movement, allow only essential services such as printing across segments and use deny-by-default firewall or access policies that block other traffic between subnets.", "set": null}, {"id": 38, "lo": "3.4.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "An office has several unused network wall jacks in its public lobby. Which of the following controls best prevents unknown devices from connecting to the network through these jacks?", "opts": ["Setting the switch ports for the unused jacks to down", "Assigning the lobby jacks to the same VLAN as the servers", "Increasing the DHCP lease time for the lobby segment", "Changing the lobby's wireless SSID"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Controls that limit connections from unknown devices include setting unused ports to down, MAC address filtering, and DHCP reservations for known devices.", "set": null}, {"id": 39, "lo": "3.4.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "What is the broadcast address of the subnet 172.16.8.64/26?", "opts": ["172.16.8.63", "172.16.8.64", "172.16.8.126", "172.16.8.127"], "ans": "D", "expl": "A /26 has 6 host bits, or 64 addresses, so the subnet runs from 172.16.8.64 (network address) to 172.16.8.127 (broadcast address). Usable hosts are .65 to .126.", "set": null}, {"id": 40, "lo": "3.5.A", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "A switch log shows thousands of new MAC addresses appearing on port 7 within one minute. Soon after, the switch begins sending all traffic out of every port. Which of the following controls would best prevent this problem?", "opts": ["Enabling WPA3 encryption on the wireless access points", "Limiting how many MAC addresses each switch port allows", "Changing the switch's default management password", "Increasing the size of the DHCP pool for the segment"], "ans": "B", "expl": "This is MAC flooding: the CAM table overflows and the switch fails open, broadcasting traffic to all nodes. MAC filtering, limiting the number and range of MAC addresses on a port, prevents it.", "set": null}, {"id": 41, "lo": "3.5.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "Sales employees carry laptops between the office, hotels, and customer sites. Which of the following firewalls is most appropriate to protect each laptop wherever it connects?", "opts": ["A perimeter firewall", "An internal firewall", "A stateless firewall at the office boundary", "A host-based firewall"], "ans": "D", "expl": "A host-based firewall is software on an individual device and is used to secure endpoints that move between networks. Perimeter and internal firewalls protect only the office network.", "set": null}, {"id": 42, "lo": "3.5.C", "skill": "2.C", "stem": "A guest wireless segment must be allowed to resolve domain names and browse secure websites, and all other inbound TCP and UDP traffic from the segment must be blocked. Which of the following ACLs meets these requirements?", "opts": ["101 ALLOW UDP 53; 102 ALLOW TCP 443; 103 DENY ALL", "101 DENY ALL; 102 ALLOW UDP 53; 103 ALLOW TCP 443", "101 ALLOW TCP 80; 102 ALLOW TCP 443; 103 DENY ALL", "101 ALLOW UDP 53; 102 DENY ALL; 103 ALLOW TCP 443"], "ans": "A", "expl": "DNS uses port 53 and HTTPS uses port 443. Because ACLs are read top to bottom, the allow rules must come before the final deny. An ACL with DENY ALL above either allow rule blocks that traffic, and an ACL that allows TCP 80 opens unencrypted HTTP and leaves out DNS.", "set": null}, {"id": 43, "lo": "3.6.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "Several switches in a school reboot briefly each afternoon when the air-conditioning system starts. A power analyzer on the switches' circuit logs voltage dips to 104 V in a 120 V system. Which of the following is the most appropriate solution?", "opts": ["Replacing every Ethernet cable that is connected to the switches", "Installing an uninterruptible power supply or moving the switches to another circuit", "Shortening the DHCP lease time on every segment of the network", "Changing the wireless channel used by every access point in the school"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Voltage below 110 V in a 120 V system can make devices restart. Solutions for voltage drops include relocating devices to a different power circuit and installing a UPS.", "set": null}, {"id": 44, "lo": "4.3.A", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "Just before the sftp command was entered, which of the following was the absolute path of the technician's local working directory?", "opts": ["/home/mreyes", "/home/mreyes/reports", "/var/log/pos", "~/reports/pos"], "ans": "B", "expl": "pwd showed /home/mreyes, and cd reports moved into the reports subdirectory, so the prompt ~/reports means /home/mreyes/reports. /var/log/pos is the remote directory on the log server.", "set": "T6"}, {"id": 45, "lo": "4.3.B", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "The technician needs a copy of pos-0614.log on the laptop. Which of the following commands should be entered at the sftp> prompt?", "opts": ["cd pos-0614.log", "get pos-0614.log", "ls pos-0614.log", "put pos-0614.log"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Within an SFTP session, get downloads a file from the remote device to the local device, and put uploads a file from the local device. cd changes the remote directory, and ls lists it.", "set": "T6"}, {"id": 46, "lo": "4.3.B", "skill": "1.D", "stem": "Which of the following best explains how this session protects the log server's credentials?", "opts": ["The password is hidden on screen, which prevents it from being sent over the network.", "SFTP sends credentials over port 21, which only the log server can read.", "SFTP uses SSH on port 22 to encrypt all data in the session, including the login credentials.", "The session is protected because both devices have private IP addresses."], "ans": "C", "expl": "SFTP uses SSH to set up a secure connection, encrypting all data, including credentials, during transfer; both use port 22. Not displaying the password is a separate on-screen precaution, and port 21 is unencrypted FTP.", "set": "T6"}, {"id": 47, "lo": "4.4.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "R1 receives a packet addressed to 10.40.2.77. Which next hop will R1 use?", "opts": ["10.40.255.2, because that route has the lower metric", "10.40.254.2, because that route has the higher metric", "203.0.113.1, because the default route has the lowest metric overall", "None, because R1 is directly connected to 10.40.2.0/24"], "ans": "A", "expl": "Both 10.40.2.0/24 routes match. When multiple next hops are available, the route with the lowest metric (2) is chosen. The default route is used only when no more specific route matches, and R1's directly connected network is 10.40.1.0/24.", "set": "T7"}, {"id": 48, "lo": "4.4.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "The link on interface Gi0/1 fails. Which of the following best describes how R1 will then send packets to 10.40.2.0/24?", "opts": ["R1 will use the route through 10.40.254.2 on Gi0/2.", "R1 will drop every packet for 10.40.2.0/24 until Gi0/1 is repaired.", "R1 will send the packets through 203.0.113.1 to the internet.", "R1 will broadcast the packets out of every interface."], "ans": "A", "expl": "Metrics and available routes change with network conditions such as device failure, which can cause data to take different paths. With Gi0/1 down, the remaining route to 10.40.2.0/24, through 10.40.254.2 (metric 5), is used.", "set": "T7"}, {"id": 49, "lo": "4.4.C", "skill": "1.C", "stem": "A technician runs tracert to a cloud server. Hops 1 through 3 respond in under 15 ms, and every line from hop 4 onward shows \"* * * Request timed out.\" Which of the following is the best conclusion?", "opts": ["The technician's computer has an APIPA address and cannot reach the internet.", "The technician's default gateway is down and is not forwarding any packets.", "The cloud server is responding normally, so there is no failure along the path.", "The path fails after hop 3, so the failure is at or beyond the fourth router."], "ans": "D", "expl": "Traceroute output can be used to count hops and identify delays and points of failure. Hops 1–3, including the gateway, respond normally, so the failure lies at or beyond hop 4.", "set": null}, {"id": 50, "lo": "4.1.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "The online ordering website of a regional restaurant chain returns \"Service unavailable\" errors on a Friday evening, and network monitoring shows an unexpected, very high volume of incoming traffic. Which part of the CIA triad is most clearly affected?", "opts": ["Availability", "Confidentiality", "Integrity", "Authentication"], "ans": "A", "expl": "A breach of availability is identified by an inability to access systems, slow or dropped connections, unexpected high traffic volume, and frequent error messages. Authentication is not part of the CIA triad.", "set": null}, {"id": 51, "lo": "4.1.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "A list of customer usernames and passwords from a gym's member portal is posted on a public website. Which part of the CIA triad was compromised?", "opts": ["Availability, because members can no longer reach the portal", "Integrity, because the member list was copied", "Confidentiality, because credentials were exposed to unauthorized people", "Integrity, because the website posting is inaccurate"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Confidentiality means only authorized individuals access data; exposed credentials are evidence of a confidentiality breach. Copying data does not alter it, so integrity is not the clearest match.", "set": null}, {"id": 52, "lo": "4.1.B", "skill": "3.A", "stem": "A community bank's online services fail several times over a few months. Which of the following is the most likely long-term consequence?", "opts": ["An increase in the bank's available bandwidth", "Reputational damage and erosion of customer trust", "Automatic replacement of the bank's routers by the ISP", "A permanent change to every customer's password"], "ans": "B", "expl": "Extended or repeated outages can cause long-term consequences: financial loss from missed transactions, reputational damage that discourages customers or investors, and erosion of public trust.", "set": null}, {"id": 53, "lo": "4.2.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "A hospital transfers patient lab results between systems, and every byte must arrive complete and in order. Which transport protocol is most appropriate, and why?", "opts": ["TCP, because it is connectionless and faster than UDP", "TCP, because it tracks acknowledgments and resends missing data", "UDP, because it resends any data that are lost", "UDP, because it guarantees that data arrive in order"], "ans": "B", "expl": "TCP is connection-oriented, tracks acknowledgments, and resends missing data, so it is used when complete and accurate transmission is essential. UDP is connectionless and is used when speed matters more than accuracy.", "set": null}, {"id": 54, "lo": "4.2.B", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "OSI layers 5, 6, and 7 correspond to which layer of the TCP/IP model?", "opts": ["Application layer", "Internet layer", "Network access layer", "Transport layer"], "ans": "A", "expl": "OSI layers 5–7, the upper layers, correlate with the TCP/IP application layer. OSI layer 4 matches transport, layer 3 matches internet, and layers 1–2 match network access.", "set": null}, {"id": 55, "lo": "4.2.C", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "A technician finds that a computer's cable and IP configuration are correct and that ping to a web server succeeds, but the web application on TCP port 443 will not load. A firewall rule is blocking port 443. At which layer of the OSI model is the problem located?", "opts": ["Physical layer", "Data link layer", "Network layer", "Transport layer"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Using the model isolates an issue to a layer. Cables are physical, MAC addresses are data link, and IP addresses and ping are network layer, all of which work. Firewalls and port-based filtering belong to the transport layer.", "set": null}, {"id": 56, "lo": "4.5.A", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "An unknown person follows an employee through a badge-controlled door and into a company's server room. Which of the following is the most likely impact of this tactic?", "opts": ["Slower wireless performance for users inside the server room", "An exhausted DHCP pool on the server segment of the network", "Direct access to devices, data theft, or equipment tampering", "Expired passwords for all of the accounts on the servers"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Tailgating bypasses physical access controls, which can allow direct access to network-connected devices, data theft, or equipment tampering.", "set": null}, {"id": 57, "lo": "4.5.B", "skill": "2.B", "stem": "A company wants to reduce the risk that unauthorized people follow employees through badge-controlled doors into secure areas. Which of the following controls would most directly reduce this risk?", "opts": ["Account lockout after five failed login attempts on every server", "A separate guest wireless network that uses a captive portal", "An access control vestibule and staff training on social engineering", "Moving the servers' IP addresses to a different subnet with a new mask"], "ans": "C", "expl": "Physical security controls against direct access include card readers and access control vestibules, which let only one person through at a time, and training staff to recognize and respond to social engineering. The other controls address network threats.", "set": null}, {"id": 58, "lo": "4.5.C", "skill": "2.A", "stem": "Which of the following best describes the difference between an intrusion detection system (IDS) and an intrusion prevention system (IPS)?", "opts": ["An IDS generates alerts about threats, while an IPS can also block suspicious traffic.", "An IDS blocks all traffic from unknown devices, while an IPS only records the traffic.", "An IDS is installed only on laptops, while an IPS is installed only on network servers.", "An IDS reviews the firewall rules, while an IPS assigns IP addresses to network hosts."], "ans": "A", "expl": "IDSs monitor traffic and generate alerts if a threat is detected; IPSs inspect traffic and can take immediate action to block suspicious traffic.", "set": null}, {"id": 59, "lo": "4.6.A", "skill": "1.A", "stem": "Which of the following statements about an IPv6 address is accurate?", "opts": ["It has 32 bits in 4 octets, which are written in dotted decimal format with periods.", "It has 48 bits and is hardcoded into each network interface card by the manufacturer.", "It has 128 bits in 8 hextets, enough for every device to have a globally unique address.", "It has 64 bits, which provides fewer possible addresses than IPv4 provides today."], "ans": "C", "expl": "IPv6 addresses have 128 bits in 8 hextets of hexadecimal digits, giving 2^128 possible addresses so every device can have a globally unique, routable address. 32 bits in 4 octets describes IPv4, and a 48-bit hardcoded identifier describes a MAC address.", "set": null}, {"id": 60, "lo": "4.6.B", "skill": "3.B", "stem": "At a distribution center, the failure of the single core switch stopped every barcode scanner for four hours. Which of the following measures would most directly prevent a repeat of this outage?", "opts": ["Changing the scanners from dynamic to static IP addresses", "Requiring stronger passwords on the scanners", "Moving the scanners to a guest wireless network", "Connecting a secondary, redundant switch"], "ans": "D", "expl": "Redundancy, including connecting secondary routers and switches, keeps backup systems available if a primary system fails. The other choices do not remove the single point of failure.", "set": null}], "frq": {"title": "Brightwater Public Library", "intro": "<p>You are a network technician for Brightwater Public Library, which is open 9:00 a.m. to 8:00 p.m. Two problems were reported on Monday morning: the circulation desk computer cannot reach the staff printer or the staff file server, and many patrons' devices cannot get online. The library director also asks you to review weekend security logs and plan a guest wireless network. Use the four sources below.</p>", "sources": [["Source 1: Switch output and patch documentation", "<pre>Switch# show vlan brief\nVLAN  Name       Status   Ports\n1     default    active   Gi0/24\n10    STAFF      active   Gi0/1, Gi0/2, Gi0/3, Gi0/4\n20    PUBLIC     active   Gi0/5, Gi0/6, Gi0/7, Gi0/8, Gi0/9,\n                          Gi0/10, Gi0/11, Gi0/12\n30    PRINTERS   active   Gi0/13, Gi0/14</pre>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>Port</th><th>Device (from patch documentation)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Gi0/1–Gi0/3</td><td>Staff office PCs</td></tr><tr><td>Gi0/4</td><td>Staff file server, 192.168.10.8 (static)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gi0/5–Gi0/8, Gi0/10–Gi0/11</td><td>Public catalog computers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gi0/9</td><td>Circulation desk PC (staff device)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gi0/12</td><td>Wireless access point for patrons</td></tr><tr><td>Gi0/13</td><td>Staff printer</td></tr></table>"], ["Source 2: Device configuration and DHCP status", "<pre>Circulation desk PC&gt; ipconfig\n   IPv4 Address. . . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.20.104\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.255.0\n   Default Gateway . . . . . . . . . : 192.168.20.1\n\nPatron laptop&gt; ipconfig\n   Autoconfiguration IPv4 Address. . : 169.254.88.3\n   Subnet Mask . . . . . . . . . . . : 255.255.0.0</pre>\n<table class=\"stim\"><tr><th>DHCP pool (VLAN 20 PUBLIC)</th><th>Value</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Address range</td><td>192.168.20.100 – 192.168.20.119 (20 addresses)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Addresses leased</td><td>20 of 20</td></tr><tr><td>Lease time</td><td>7 days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical patron devices per day</td><td>About 60, most of which visit only once a week</td></tr></table>"], ["Source 3: IDS log excerpt (Saturday)", "<pre>Time      Source           Destination       Event\n21:47:10  192.168.20.115   192.168.10.0/24   Port scan detected (TCP 1-1024)\n21:52:33  192.168.20.115   192.168.10.8      SSH login failed (user: admin) x35\n21:58:02  192.168.20.115   203.0.113.200     Connection to known malicious IP\n22:05:44  192.168.20.115   192.168.10.8      SSH login failed (user: staff) x22</pre>\n<p>No firewall or access rules currently restrict traffic between VLAN 20 and VLAN 10.</p>"], ["Source 4: Library network policy (excerpt)", "<ol class=\"policy\">\n<li><b>L1 Staff devices.</b> All staff devices must be on the STAFF VLAN.</li>\n<li><b>L2 Separation.</b> Public and guest devices must not reach staff systems. Printing is the only service allowed between segments.</li>\n<li><b>L3 Guest wireless.</b> The guest network must use WPA2 or WPA3, require patrons to accept the terms of use, and limit each session to 2 hours.</li>\n<li><b>L4 Growth.</b> The library will add IPv6 addressing next year.</li></ol>"]], "parts": [{"label": "(a)", "pts": 3, "lo": "4.5.D, 3.6.B", "prompt": "Using Sources 1 and 2, explain why the circulation desk computer cannot reach the staff file server. Then describe the switch configuration change that would fix the problem, and describe how you would verify the fix.", "rubric": ["1 point: Explains that port Gi0/9 is assigned to VLAN 20 (PUBLIC), so the circulation PC received a 192.168.20.x address on the public segment and cannot reach VLAN 10 devices, because devices on different VLANs cannot communicate unless routing is configured.", "1 point: Describes configuring Gi0/9 as an access port assigned to VLAN 10 (STAFF), so the PC joins the staff segment (and then renews its IP address).", "1 point: Describes a verification, such as running show vlan brief to confirm Gi0/9 is listed under VLAN 10 and pinging the staff file server at 192.168.10.8 from the circulation PC."], "sample": "Source 1 shows that Gi0/9, the circulation desk port, is in VLAN 20 PUBLIC, and Source 2 confirms the PC has the public address 192.168.20.104. Devices on different VLANs cannot communicate unless routing is configured, so it cannot reach the staff server on VLAN 10. I would configure Gi0/9 as an access port in VLAN 10 and have the PC renew its address. To verify, I would run show vlan brief to confirm Gi0/9 is now listed under VLAN 10 and then ping 192.168.10.8 from the circulation PC."}, {"label": "(b)", "pts": 2, "lo": "3.6.B, 3.6.C", "prompt": "Identify the most likely root cause of the patron laptop's problem, citing evidence from Source 2. Then determine one solution, and justify why it would resolve the problem.", "rubric": ["1 point: Identifies an exhausted DHCP pool as the root cause, citing the APIPA address 169.254.88.3 and 20 of 20 addresses leased.", "1 point: Determines an appropriate solution, such as expanding the DHCP pool range or shortening the 7-day lease, and justifies it (for example, about 60 devices a day but only 20 addresses; one-time visitors keep addresses for 7 days, so a shorter lease recycles them quickly)."], "sample": "The DHCP pool is exhausted: the laptop has the APIPA address 169.254.88.3, and all 20 of 20 addresses are leased. About 60 devices a day connect but most visit only weekly, so the 7-day lease keeps addresses tied up by devices that have left. Shortening the lease to a few hours would recycle those addresses, and expanding the range (for example, to .100–.250) would also give enough addresses for a day's patrons."}, {"label": "(c)", "pts": 2, "lo": "4.5.C, 3.4.B", "prompt": "Identify two indicators of a security threat in Source 3. Then describe one control, consistent with policy L2, that would limit the impact of this activity, and explain how it works.", "rubric": ["1 point: Identifies two different threat indicators, such as the unauthorized port scan of the staff segment, repeated failed SSH logins (35 and 22 attempts), the connection to a known malicious IP, or activity outside business hours (after the 8:00 p.m. closing).", "1 point: Describes a control consistent with L2, such as a deny-by-default firewall or access rules between VLAN 20 and VLAN 10 that permit only printing traffic, and explains that it blocks public devices from reaching staff systems and limits lateral movement."], "sample": "Two indicators are the port scan of 192.168.10.0/24 at 21:47, which occurred after the library closed at 8:00 p.m., and the 35 failed SSH logins to the staff file server at 21:52. Under policy L2, I would add deny-by-default access rules between VLAN 20 and VLAN 10 that permit only printing traffic. A public device could then not even reach the staff server's SSH port, which stops lateral movement from the public segment."}, {"label": "(d)", "pts": 2, "lo": "3.3.D, 1.4.C", "prompt": "Describe how you would configure the patrons' guest wireless network so that it meets policy L3 and policies L1 and L2.", "rubric": ["1 point: Describes creating a separate guest SSID with a strong password and WPA2 or WPA3 encryption, and disabling the guest network's access to the internal network (staff systems).", "1 point: Describes enabling a captive portal that requires accepting the terms of use and setting a 2-hour session time limit."], "sample": "On the access point, I would enable the guest network feature, create a separate SSID such as Brightwater-Guest, set WPA3 (or at least WPA2) with a strong password, and disable guest access to the internal network so patrons reach only the internet. I would enable a captive portal where patrons must accept the terms of use and set a session limit of 2 hours. I would test it by connecting a device and confirming internet access but no access to 192.168.10.8."}, {"label": "(e)", "pts": 2, "lo": "4.6.A", "prompt": "For policy L4, the library's new server will use the IPv6 address 2001:0DB8:00A0:0000:0000:0000:0000:0005. Write this address in compressed format, and explain one way IPv6 supports the library's growth.", "rubric": ["1 point: Writes 2001:DB8:A0::5.", "1 point: Explains a valid benefit, such as 128-bit addressing (2^128 addresses) giving every device a globally unique, routable address so the library will not run out of addresses, or more efficient transmission from simplified headers and routing."], "sample": "The compressed address is 2001:DB8:A0::5: leading zeros are dropped (0DB8 → DB8, 00A0 → A0, 0005 → 5) and the four all-zero hextets become ::. IPv6 uses 128-bit addresses, so there are enough addresses for every new device the library adds to have its own globally unique address."}]}};
